              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 18-year-old male patient, admitted on 17/01/2012 with acute right iliac fossa pain, nausea, and low-grade fever suggestive of appendicitis. Clinical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Blood tests confirmed elevated inflammatory markers, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was kept nil by mouth, received intravenous fluids, analgesia, and prophylactic antibiotics. On 18/01/2012, he successfully underwent laparoscopic appendectomy without intraoperative complications. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Wound care was performed, and the patient was mobilised early. He was discharged home on 20/01/2012 in good general condition with oral analgesia and wound care instructions. Please arrange follow-up in approximately two weeks. Do not hesitate to contact me should any concerns arise.</div>"
            },
